WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:05.850
Although Albert Einstein's intelligence far
exceeded the average, his brain was, intriguingly,

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.570
smaller than the average man's.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:11.620
A typical adult male brain weighs around 3
pounds (1,400 g)

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.400
Einstein’s weighed 2.64 pounds (1,197 g)

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.789
While smaller overall, a crucial section of
his brain was 15% wider than average.

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:25.480
The inferior parietal lobe is the region of
the brain associated with visual and spatial

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.480
reasoning.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.070
This brain area would have been crucial to
coming up with Einstein’s famous theory

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:35.520
of general relativity, which describes how
a massive object can bend space-time around

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.600
it, much like how planets move in a curved
path around the sun.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.840
In 1999, a team at Canada’s McMaster University
led by neuroscientist Sandra Witelson discovered

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.550
his parietal lobe was wider than normal because
it lacked a groove found in normal brains.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.840
The Sylvian fissure, also known as the lateral
sulcus, usually separates the frontal and

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.370
parietal lobes from the temporal lobe.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:05.390
However, in Einstein’s brain, this big crevice
was largely absent; the rear end was shorter

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:06.390
or truncated.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.400
It did not extend as far into the parietal
lobe as it does in most people's brains.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.990
This expanded Einstein’s parietal lobe,
making it wider than normal.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:21.190
When Einstein died in 1955 at a New Jersey
hospital, the pathologist who performed the

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:24.650
autopsy removed his brain and kept it for
himself.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:29.799
Dr. Thomas Harvey later convinced Einstein’s
son to allow him to keep it for research.

00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:32.110
These labels were added by Harvey.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:37.130
He cut Einstein’s brain into 240 blocks
and stored them in a cardboard box in his

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:41.750
office in a mason jar and in a cookie jar
preserved in gelatinous material.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:46.630
Dr. Harvey procrastinated and never actually
published any research.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:52.480
He did create 2,400 slides containing thin
sections of Einstein’s brain tissue to be

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.539
examined under a microscope and sent them
off to other scientists who revealed fascinating

00:01:57.539 --> 00:01:59.000
insights.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.719
In 2001, Dr. Dahlia Zaidel of the University
of California, Los Angeles, examined two slices

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:10.069
of Einstein’s brain containing the hippocampus,
which plays a crucial role in learning and

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:11.069
memory.

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:14.810
She found that the neurons on the left side
of Einstein's hippocampus were larger than

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.260
those on the right.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.069
The left hemisphere is primarily responsible
for logical thinking, analytical processing,

00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:23.709
and mathematical abilities.

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:28.200
Larger neurons in the left hippocampus suggested
stronger nerve cell connections between the

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.489
hippocampus and the neocortex, which is the
command center of the brain involved in high-level

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:34.530
thinking.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:38.950
In addition to the asymmetry in Einstein's
hippocampus, researchers also found that the

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:43.190
bundle of nerve fibers connecting the two
hemispheres of his brain called the corpus

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:45.700
callosum was thicker than average.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:51.569
In 2013, researchers from China and the US
found that Einstein's corpus callosum, denoted

00:02:51.569 --> 00:02:56.930
in red, was thicker than those in the control
group of elderly men denoted in blue, and

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:02.680
also thicker than most of those in the younger
control group of men 24 to 30 years old, denoted

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.200
in green.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.620
This increased thickness likely facilitated
better communication between the left and

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:13.370
right hemispheres, contributing to Einstein's
exceptional cognitive abilities.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:18.910
It can be likened to a wider bridge between
two cities, enabling faster transportation

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.310
of people and goods and improved overall connectivity.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:27.959
Dr. Harvey also sent off four slices of Einstein’s
brain in a mayonnaise jar to neuroscientist

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:31.019
Marian Diamond of the University of California
Berkeley

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:36.700
Her study, published in 1985, concluded that
Einstein’s brain had more support cells

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:37.970
than average.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:43.120
These red and blue structures are called glial
cells - aptly named from the Greek word 'glee-ull',

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.250
meaning 'glue'.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.800
They play a critical role in nourishing and
protecting the neurons.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.370
Additional protection might have allowed his
neurons to function more effectively.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:56.560
Remarkably, Einstein’s brain didn’t show
any signs of aging.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.930
Even at the age of 76, his brain had no traces
of lipofuscin, the fine yellow-brown pigment

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:07.830
granules associated with "wear and tear" in
the human brain, similar to how an old car

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.879
might accumulate rust over time.

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:14.790
It’s believed Einstein was born this way
rather than being influenced by environmental

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:15.790
factors.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.410
But it doesn’t mean our brains can’t change.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:23.560
Dr. Diamond was the first to show that the
brain can change and improve at ANY age.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:28.110
Her work with rats demonstrated that those
who played with mazes, ladders, wheels, and

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:33.030
their companions had larger brain cells and
a greater amount of glial cells compared to

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.370
rats that were kept isolated with no toys.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:41.690
The implication is that all animals, including
humans, benefit from an enriched environment,

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.810
and impoverished environments can lower the
capacity to learn.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.139
This is now called plasticity of the brain.

00:04:48.139 --> 00:04:52.850
And it has powerful implications for what
we’re capable of achieving.

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:57.380
According to Einstein’s wishes, his body
was cremated, and his ashes were scattered

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.720
in a secret spot by the Delaware River.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.540
He didn’t want his body to be worshipped.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:08.130
But Einstein’s biographer said that, “He
had insisted his brain be used for research…”

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:14.520
Even after nearly 70 years, Einstein’s brain
still has so much to teach the world.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:19.360
Just like Einstein's brain, our own genetic
makeup holds secrets about who we are.

